{
  "image_name": "1fab552d78aa4f33122bf51e57bb7810.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a cell releases different kinds of extracellular vesicles and how this changes in healthy versus dying cells. In the upper, healthy-cell region, two main routes are shown. One route is direct budding outward from the plasma membrane, producing ectosomes that carry visible DNA, RNA, and proteins. The other route begins with an early endosome that matures into a multivesicular endosome (MVE); this compartment either fuses with the cell surface to release exosomes or is sent to lysosomes for degradation after fusion with a primary lysosome. The endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi appear as sources feeding the lysosomal pathway.\n\nAcross these pathways, the vesicles are depicted as small membrane-bound spheres containing different combinations of nucleic acids and proteins, emphasizing that cargo is selectively packaged and exported.\n\nIn the lower, dying-cell region, the membrane appears to bleb and fragment into much larger apoptotic bodies. These bodies contain bulk cellular material, including mitochondria and nucleic acids, rather than the smaller, more uniform vesicles released by healthy cells. Overall, the image contrasts controlled vesicle secretion in living cells with membrane fragmentation and apoptotic body formation during cell death.",
